1
00:00:00,367 --> 00:00:02,250
Graffitty is a terminal emulator,

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:04,217
with a tiling manager and Chromium.

3
00:00:07,117 --> 00:00:08,717
But if you zoom out, you'll notice

4
00:00:08,717 --> 00:00:10,533
there's much more space to work with.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,983
Each pane shows CPU, RAM usage,

6
00:00:13,983 --> 00:00:15,470
and agent status.

7
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,400
Stash a pane outside the main view.

8
00:00:19,517 --> 00:00:21,717
You can return to the main view at any time.

9
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:24,467
Or jump back to your previous view

10
00:00:24,467 --> 00:00:25,967
and take a closer look at any pane.

11
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:34,067
The builtin filetree is synchronised

12
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:35,650
with the shell's current working directory.

13
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:42,283
You can edit the file name like editing a vim buffer,

14
00:00:42,283 --> 00:00:43,570
and save to apply it.

15
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:51,267
Opening the files opens Neovim,

16
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:54,200
or Chromium to preview all kinds of media.

17
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,300
You can also send file paths to the terminal,

18
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,877
allowing you to change directory or open files.

19
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:10,750
Get creative with Chromium.

20
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:12,617
It's a versatile viewer that handles things

21
00:01:12,617 --> 00:01:15,117
traditional terminals often need workarounds for.

22
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:18,133
For example, use the MarkdownPreview plugin

23
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:20,433
in Neovim to open Graffitty Chromium.

24
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:22,367
Now you have a live Markdown preview

25
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:24,017
right inside your terminal.

26
00:01:28,867 --> 00:01:30,917
Ask Claude to explain with a diagram.

27
00:01:30,917 --> 00:01:33,817
Graffitty Chromium will open right beside your terminal.

28
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:47,317
Multiple agents can each use

29
00:01:47,317 --> 00:01:49,133
an isolated Chromium session,

30
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:51,530
so they can run end-to-end tests in parallel.

31
00:01:57,233 --> 00:01:59,417
You can also use Chromium's native interface

32
00:01:59,417 --> 00:02:00,717
in a separate window.

33
00:02:00,883 --> 00:02:02,767
Closing that window doesn't close Chromium.

34
00:02:02,767 --> 00:02:05,417
It simply brings the same chromium back into Graffitty.

35
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,833
One thing I find frustrating about agentic coding

36
00:02:11,933 --> 00:02:14,867
is how quickly agents can open a jumble of windows.

37
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:17,100
They get in the way, steal focus,

38
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:20,550
and make it hard to tell which agent is using which browser.

39
00:02:20,683 --> 00:02:22,717
That's why Graffitty shows clear ownership

40
00:02:22,717 --> 00:02:24,183
with a connecting line.

41
00:02:24,183 --> 00:02:26,133
You can move chromiums out of the main view

42
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:27,747
and still see who's using them.

43
00:02:30,917 --> 00:02:33,583
The down arrow gives you a summary of every tab,

44
00:02:33,583 --> 00:02:35,917
including its panes, CPU and RAM usage,

45
00:02:35,917 --> 00:02:37,117
and agent status.

46
00:02:37,117 --> 00:02:40,517
Type to fuzzy search tab and pane titles.

47
00:02:42,383 --> 00:02:45,033
Aren't you already used to Vim's command line?

48
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:46,200
The same thing is here,

49
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:47,983
with a fuzzy finder built in.

50
00:02:47,983 --> 00:02:49,083
You no longer have to keep

51
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:51,317
every terminal feature as a key map.

52
00:02:51,317 --> 00:02:53,667
Search commands, switch tabs, or jump to a pane.

53
00:02:54,867 --> 00:02:56,717
Just like tmux keeps your session alive

54
00:02:56,717 --> 00:02:58,150
on an SSH server,

55
00:02:58,150 --> 00:02:59,533
you can detach from Graffitty

56
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:01,150
and attach again any time.

57
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:02,733
It even has mobile clients,

58
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:04,250
so you can code wherever you are.

59
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:10,867
Let me explain how the graph feature works.

60
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:13,683
Each terminal pane can advertise itself as a node,

61
00:03:13,683 --> 00:03:15,867
so it can queue messages to another pane.

62
00:03:16,167 --> 00:03:19,283
For example, Codex on the left implements a feature

63
00:03:19,283 --> 00:03:21,433
and sends its work to Claude in the middle.

64
00:03:22,033 --> 00:03:24,000
Claude only sends to the Slack node

65
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,000
when a condition is met

66
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,150
that the user needs to know about.

67
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:36,333
Now ask Codex to implement the website.

68
00:03:38,083 --> 00:03:39,167
When Codex finishes,

69
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:41,880
Claude receives its session and checks the work.

70
00:03:48,617 --> 00:03:50,167
Claude finds missing requirements

71
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:51,417
and sends the review to Slack.

72
00:03:54,817 --> 00:03:56,433
Graph is very useful when you want

73
00:03:56,433 --> 00:03:58,167
to quickly prototype a pipeline.

74
00:03:58,317 --> 00:04:00,117
This SystemOne node that calls

75
00:04:00,117 --> 00:04:01,433
the JEV decision model in the middle

76
00:04:01,433 --> 00:04:03,000
will route the session for you

77
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,150
based on your question!

78
00:04:15,233 --> 00:04:16,433
The parallel node waits

79
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,073
until every agent finishes.

80
00:04:23,267 --> 00:04:24,467
Once all three finish,

81
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:26,750
it sends their results and session references

82
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:27,900
together to the reviewer.

83
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,417
You can even make your own custom node

84
00:04:33,417 --> 00:04:36,017
very easily with Graffitty Graph Protocol.

85
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:38,967
First, let's use the Payload Inspector node,

86
00:04:38,967 --> 00:04:41,013
which shows what data are sent through the graph.

87
00:04:41,817 --> 00:04:43,717
It sends just enough information

88
00:04:43,717 --> 00:04:45,150
for the next node to process,

89
00:04:45,150 --> 00:04:47,933
like the session file path and the last message.

90
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:51,017
Here is a minimal Python example

91
00:04:51,017 --> 00:04:52,683
that uses the GGP protocol

92
00:04:52,683 --> 00:04:55,217
to convert the data to uppercase.

93
00:04:56,333 --> 00:04:58,300
Define the input and output ports,

94
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:00,483
process the data, and send it on.

95
00:05:01,733 --> 00:05:02,150
Run it.

96
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:03,960
Connect the nodes,

97
00:05:05,717 --> 00:05:06,627
Send the message.

98
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:14,683
This node converts the message to uppercase.

99
00:05:14,683 --> 00:05:17,000
The Payload Inspector shows the result.

100
00:05:17,083 --> 00:05:17,843
That's it.

101
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:22,950
This makes your graph pipeline versatile.

102
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:24,767
For example, create a simple watcher node

103
00:05:24,767 --> 00:05:27,450
that triggers your agent when a condition is met.

104
00:05:33,967 --> 00:05:35,437
Connect the watcher to Claude.

105
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:37,667
A new email arrives,

106
00:05:37,667 --> 00:05:40,367
and Claude makes a podcast just as it's told to!
